  "texts": [
    {
      "tag": "h1",
      "text": "We Are Shaped And Fashioned By What We Love",
      "box": {
        "x": 48,
        "y": 709,
        "w": 294,
        "h": 86
      },
      "font": "adobe-caslon-w01-smbd, serif",
      "size": "26px",
      "weight": "400",
      "style": "normal",
      "lineHeight": "28.6px",
      "letterSpacing": "normal",
      "color": "rgb(15, 35, 6)",
      "align": "center",
      "transform": "capitalize",
      "decoration": "none"
    },
    {
      "tag": "span",
      "text": "This painting of my converse in black and white attempts to show them colored on, stained, beat up and adored. I wear them everywhere, all the time, and I feel that they are a reflection of the deepest parts of me. The song lyrics, the quotes, the doodles, and so many other things about them are parts of me I share with the world. I love that I can carry a tiny bit of my art with me wherever I go. This painting is meant to reflect the quote \"we are shaped and fashioned by what we love\" from the book \"Steal Like an Artist\". To me, this quote is a description of what happens when you find something that resonates with you, whether it be a song, an animal, a shape, or a color, that that thing will continue to appear in your life. When we love things we allow them to make us into who we are. My converse are a collection of the things that mean the most to me.",
      "box": {
        "x": 49,
        "y": 807,
        "w": 293,
        "h": 631
      },
      "font": "alfabet, sans-serif",
      "size": "18px",
      "weight": "400",
      "style": "normal",
      "lineHeight": "23.4px",
      "letterSpacing": "normal",
      "color": "rgb(15, 35, 6)",
      "align": "center",
      "transform": "none",
      "decoration": "none"
    },
    {
      "tag": "h1",
      "text": "Technical Process",
      "box": {
        "x": 24,
        "y": 2737,
        "w": 342,
        "h": 29
      },
      "font": "adobe-caslon-w01-smbd, serif",
      "size": "26px",
      "weight": "400",
      "style": "normal",
      "lineHeight": "28.6px",
      "letterSpacing": "normal",
      "color": "rgb(15, 35, 6)",
      "align": "center",
      "transform": "capitalize",
      "decoration": "none"
    },
    {
      "tag": "span",
      "text": "For this piece, I started with a blank canvas and I sketched out the photo I took of my own shoes. I then painted over it with acrylic paints, using almost entirely black and white (plus a little brown), to get the tones I wanted. I attempted to establish enough contrast to make the scene catch the viewer's eye, and to add deep enough shadows that the design was almost pulled off the page. after painting, I used a white chalk pencil to sketch the white designs on the shoe, and I ended up liking the texture and not painting over it like I originally planned. I then used a black thin sharpie to do the black details on the shoe, and I sealed the painting with a matte varnish.",
      "box": {
        "x": 25,
        "y": 2777,
        "w": 340,
        "h": 420
      },
      "font": "alfabet, sans-serif",
      "size": "18px",
      "weight": "400",
      "style": "normal",
      "lineHeight": "23.4px",
      "letterSpacing": "normal",
      "color": "rgb(15, 35, 6)",
      "align": "center",
      "transform": "none",
      "decoration": "none"
    },
    {
      "tag": "h1",
      "text": "Elements And Principles",
      "box": {
        "x": 24,
        "y": 3303,
        "w": 342,
        "h": 29
      },
      "font": "adobe-caslon-w01-smbd, serif",
      "size": "26px",
      "weight": "400",
      "style": "normal",
      "lineHeight": "28.6px",
      "letterSpacing": "normal",
      "color": "rgb(15, 35, 6)",
      "align": "center",
      "transform": "capitalize",
      "decoration": "none"
    },
    {
      "tag": "p",
      "text": "The primary element of art I used in this piece was value, especially through my use of almost exclusively black and white. For elements of design, I used mostly emphasis in order to make the shoes stand out on the stark white background.",
